# Who may sign a release tag of this repository, in the format git's
# gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile reads (ssh-keygen(1), ALLOWED SIGNERS).
#
# .github/workflows/release.yml verifies every pushed v* tag against this file,
# through .github/release/verify-tag.sh, and creates no release for a tag it
# cannot verify. The same workflow shows the verifier refusing a tag that is
# unsigned, lightweight, signed by a key not named here, or carrying a
# signature made for another tag, on every pull request and before every
# release.
#
# What this file cannot do: it travels in the same tree as the tag it vouches
# for, so whoever can cut a v* tag can point one at a tree that trusts their own
# key. A check against it shows that the key named here signed the tag, and
# nothing about who holds that key.
#
# The principal is the address this repository's commits already carry as
# author, so this file publishes nothing they did not.
#
# ROTATION, edited in the same change that starts using the new key:
#   - RETIRED key: keep its line and add valid-before="YYYYMMDD" at least two
#     days after the UTC date of the last tag it signed. git checks a key
#     against the tag's own date, so the tags it signed keep verifying, and
#     deleting the line un-verifies all of them. Two days, because a bare date
#     is midnight at the start of that day in the verifier's time zone.
#   - COMPROMISED key: delete its line. A tag's date is whatever its signer
#     wrote, so valid-before cannot fence off a stolen key; the tags it signed
#     stop verifying, which is the honest state for them.
